jennifer machado used to run a soup  kitchen from her home to feed hungry  children in her neighborhood but not  anymore  when she refused to display a  pro-government poster in her house after  government losses in september's primary  elections her food supplies dried up  local media reports of her defiance had  apparently reached the highest levels  just weeks before sunday's far more  important legislative elections  they brought us these posters for us to  receive food and give it to the people  some people had no problem in taking  pictures with these posters but others  did not want to and we couldn't force  them everything they gave us we had to  take pictures now they took all the help  away from us and never brought food  again and now we're closed  jennifer lives in general rodriguez a  poor neighborhood about 40 minutes away  from the capital she has four children  the government launched a series of  measures to revert the setbacks it  suffered during the primary elections  last september those results show that  the government lost lots of support in  poor neighborhoods like this one and  that's why they started distributing  cash food among other things but polls  suggest it may not be working in their  favor  in addition to the poster jennifer says  she was also given a refrigerator three  days after the elections she says it was  an attempt to get her vote  in sunday's legislative elections people  will vote to renew half the chamber of  deputies and a third of the senate  at stake is president alberto fernandez  ability to govern effectively for the  remaining two years of his term marked  so far by economic hardship worsened by  the coveted 19 pandemic  the opposition says the government is  desperate to get votes  president alberto fernandez says his  priority is those in need  i've heard people tell me that inflation  worries them and i've also heard them  tell me their income is not enough and  so we listened and we changed and we  increased cash handouts so that those  with kids can improve their living and  have better conditions  argentina is currently negotiating an  almost 50 billion dollar loan with the  imf  inflation is close to 50 percent a year  and exchange rate controls make it  difficult for the economy to grow  divisions within the government on how  much money to spend on the streets have  heard the ruling coalition between  president alberto fernandez and his  powerful vice president christina  fernandez the kushner  but analysts say the biggest problem is  that argentina has no economic plan  the economy is disorganized investments  low so there's no drivers for the  economy they want to exacerbate  consumption but it means more inflation  or an impact on the exchange rate  difference that generates chaos the  government's adrift they change their  mind constantly one day they say they'll  negotiate with the imf then they won't  so who knows  argentina has been able to contain  social discontent using cash  distribution programs to assist those in  need but the elections show people want  much more they need jobs and prosperity  and the opportunity to change their  lives teresa al jazeera buenos aires
 

